    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Roles and the clerk limit are in place, and `bin/e2e` passes all 4 acceptance tests.\n\n**What it does**\n- **Roles:** the sheet can give a user name the role `clerk` or `steward`. Any name without one is a clerk. Any other role, such as `boss`, is refused with an error and nothing is stored. Each accepted change writes `ROLE_SET \u2026 user=<name> role=<role>` to the audit file.\n- **Clerk limit:** a sheet setting with two decimals; leaving it blank means no limit. Every save writes `SETTING_CHANGED \u2026 key=clerk-limit value=<limit>`, even if the value didn't change. The spec expects that: one test saves `1.50` twice and expects two lines.\n- **Settling:** if a limit is set and a clerk tries to settle a claim whose due is above it, they get an error on the claim. A due exactly at the limit can be settled. Stewards can settle any claim. The rule that whoever opened a claim can't settle it still applies to everyone, stewards included.\n\n**Where the changes are**\n- **Stored data:** migration `V18__roles_clerk_limit.sql` adds a `clerk_limit` column to the sheet and a new `sheet_role` table.\n- **Server:** a new `settings` package handles the settings and roles, at `/api/sheets/{id}/settings` and `/api/sheets/{id}/roles`. The limit check is in `ClaimService.changeStatus` and runs before anything is written, so a refused settle leaves the store and audit file untouched.\n- **Front end:** a new Settings section with the clerk-limit form and the roles form and table. It is re-read every time the sheet loads. The claim error message now says whether the problem is the limit or that you opened the claim yourself.\n\n**One rule I decided myself:** the request doesn't say what happens when a claim's due shows \"n/a\" (no rate in force for it) and a limit is set. I treat it as not \"at or below the limit\", so a clerk can't settle it. The tests don't cover this case. If you'd rather clerks could settle those claims, it's a one-line change in `requireMaySettle`.",
